Here are the PostgreSQL commands you will commonly use when working with your Docker PostgreSQL setup.

## 1. Connect to PostgreSQL

From PowerShell:

```powershell
docker exec -it infra-postgres-1 psql -U aiuser -d aimonitor
```

---

## 2. Exit PostgreSQL shell

Inside `psql`:

```sql
\q
```

---

# Database commands

### 3. Show current database

```sql
SELECT current_database();
```

---

### 4. Show current user

```sql
SELECT current_user;
```

---

### 5. List all databases

```sql
\l
```

or:

```sql
\list
```

---

### 6. Connect to another database

```sql
\c database_name
```

Example:

```sql
\c aimonitor
```

---

# Table commands

### 7. List tables

```sql
\dt
```

---

### 8. List all tables with details

```sql
\dt+
```

---

### 9. Describe a table structure

```sql
\d table_name
```

Example:

```sql
\d users
```

Shows:

* columns
* data types
* indexes
* constraints

---

### 10. List all database objects

```sql
\d
```

---

# Data commands

### 11. View table data

```sql
SELECT * FROM table_name;
```

Example:

```sql
SELECT * FROM users;
```

---

### 12. Insert data

```sql
INSERT INTO users(name,email)
VALUES ('John','john@test.com');
```

---

### 13. Update data

```sql
UPDATE users
SET name='Mike'
WHERE id=1;
```

---

### 14. Delete data

```sql
DELETE FROM users
WHERE id=1;
```

---

# Creating database objects

### 15. Create a table

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT,
    email TEXT
);
```

---

### 16. Drop a table

```sql
DROP TABLE users;
```

---

# Useful PostgreSQL information

### 17. Show PostgreSQL version

```sql
SELECT version();
```

---

### 18. Show all users

```sql
\du
```

---

### 19. Show active connections

```sql
SELECT * FROM pg_stat_activity;
```

---

### 20. Clear terminal screen

Inside `psql`:

```sql
\! cls
```

(Windows)

or:

```sql
\! clear
```

(Linux/Mac)

---

# Docker-related commands you will use often

### Check PostgreSQL container status

```powershell
docker ps
```

---

### View PostgreSQL logs

```powershell
docker logs infra-postgres-1
```

---

### Restart PostgreSQL

```powershell
docker restart infra-postgres-1
```

---

### Stop PostgreSQL

```powershell
docker stop infra-postgres-1
```

---

### Start PostgreSQL

```powershell
docker start infra-postgres-1
```

---

## For your `aimonitor` project, the commands you will probably use most often are:

```powershell
docker exec -it infra-postgres-1 psql -U aiuser -d aimonitor
```

then:

```sql
\dt
\d table_name
SELECT * FROM table_name;
INSERT INTO table_name (...);
UPDATE table_name SET ...;
\q
```

Those cover around 90% of day-to-day PostgreSQL development work.
